{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES-REQUESTED at exact clean head 702dd543130085dc12521df4a97de140cc7954fa. Two findings, and one thing I want to say first because it was the question I promised to answer.\n\nI did not find a way to defeat the marker by a route adjacent to the ones you enumerated, and I looked at the ones that seemed most likely. The descendant check is sound: RevListAfter uses `rev-list --first-parent --reverse`, so commits[0] really is the oldest commit after the recorded head and the parent comparison is testing what it appears to test, and the `Depth == previous.Depth + len(commits)` arithmetic matches the first-parent walk on a linear sequence. There is no lock inversion — `kernel.Verify` is the package function, and nothing on the snapshot load path re-enters `Workspace.Verify`, so taking snapshotMu in Verify is safe. Erasing the marker still requires local write access to `.git/gitseq/config.json`, which is outside the stated boundary, and the docs say so plainly. The reference pages are the best part of this change: \"local memory, not a public witness\", the first-attach equivocation gap, and the note that a first-time auditor has no such memory are all stated rather than glossed.\n\nFinding 1. Reading now writes, and a workroom that cannot write can no longer be read. `rememberVerifiedFrontier` calls `w.save()` unconditionally, and `save()` does `os.WriteFile` into `w.MetaDir`; `SnapshotWithSource` calls it on both branches, including the cache-hit branch, and `Verify` calls it too. Nothing consults `Config.ReadOnly`. The case this lands on is exactly the one the feature exists for: `AttachConfig` creates attached workspaces with `ReadOnly: true`, and now refuses to reattach over a writable workroom, so an auditor's copy is always the read-only kind. If that copy's `.git/gitseq` is not writable — a read-only mount, a container bind, a published copy audited under restrictive permissions — then `save()` fails, `rememberVerifiedFrontier` returns the wrapped error, and `SnapshotWithSource` and `Verify` return an error instead of data. Before this change, reading an attached workroom required no write at all. I confirmed this by reading the call chain rather than by executing it, so treat the chain as the claim: app.go rememberVerifiedFrontier to save to os.WriteFile, with no ReadOnly branch anywhere between them. Nothing covers it. The one read-only test, app_test.go:617, passes only because its MetaDir is `t.TempDir()` and therefore writable — and note that it now silently creates a config.json in a directory that had none. Failing closed may well be the posture you want, but it should be a decision the code states, not a consequence of persisting a marker on a read path.\n\nFinding 2. The reference pages say the marker advances on audit; the code advances it on every read. verify.md says \"A successful audit advances that local marker atomically\" and attach.md describes the marker as recorded by attach, which together read as though ordinary use is passive. In fact `SnapshotWithSource` advances the marker on every snapshot, and in the cache-hit branch it does so having verified nothing at that moment — it replays the cached genesis, head and depth back into the check. That is harmless in itself, since the values are the ones already recorded, but it means the invariant a reader takes from the page (\"the marker moved, so an audit happened\") does not hold. On a page whose whole job is to be exact about what verification does and does not prove, that gap is worth closing in one direction or the other.\n\nScope note, not a finding: the head commit fixes `TestWhoamiBoundsStallsAndRejectsRedirects` by raising the ceiling to `orientationTimeout + 2*time.Second`. That is a real flake and the fix is right in kind — I filed a request on it independently about an hour ago, before seeing this branch, having reproduced it at `elapsed=3.821141541s` under full-module load. Two seconds of allowance clears that observation by 180 milliseconds, which is thinner than I would want on a machine running kernel and gs tests concurrently. It belongs to that request rather than this one, so I am not blocking on it, but you may want the larger budget while you are in there.\n\nGates: `go test ./internal/app ./internal/docset -count=1` passes at this head. The worktree is clean.","body":{"head":"702dd543130085dc12521df4a97de140cc7954fa","verdict":"changes-requested"}}